=== L5 Salamis & Naval Warfare ===
id: 919fad5e-4b39-43a6-9bb0-93d6abda84b6
slug: salamis-naval-warfare
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:41:37.581883+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The sea battle that saved Greece — the trireme, Salamis and how sea power decided the Persian Wars.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Athens and the Sea</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The battle that truly saved Greece from Persian conquest was not fought on land but on the water. In 480 BC, the same year as Thermopylae, the Greek and Persian fleets clashed in the narrow strait between the island of Salamis and the Attic coastline. The result &mdash; a decisive Greek victory &mdash; shattered Persian naval power and made it impossible for Xerxes to supply or reinforce his army effectively. Without that victory at sea, the land battles that followed could never have ended Greek resistance.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Athens was central to the Greek naval effort. In the years before 480 BC, the Athenian statesman <dfn class="term" data-def="The Athenian statesman and general who persuaded Athens to build a large fleet with silver from the Laurion mines and devised the strategy of luring the Persian fleet into the narrow strait at Salamis.">Themistocles</dfn> had persuaded the Athenian assembly to invest a windfall of silver from the mines at Laurion in building a fleet of two hundred <dfn class="term" data-def="A fast, oar-powered Greek warship with three banks of oars and a crew of around 170 rowers; the dominant warship of the fifth century BC.">triremes</dfn>. This decision transformed Athens into a major naval power and ultimately into the city that would save Greece.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The Trireme: Design and Crew</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The trireme was the warship of its age. Its name refers to its three banks of oars, each bank rowed by a different tier of men. A trireme was roughly thirty-seven metres long and six metres wide, built of lightweight fir wood for speed rather than durability. The hull tapered to a sharp bronze ram at the prow &mdash; this was the trireme&rsquo;s primary weapon. The vessel could be fitted with a mast and sail for open-water cruising but fought entirely under oar power, where it achieved remarkable speed.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">A full crew numbered around two hundred men. Of these, about one hundred and seventy were rowers &mdash; the thalamites (lowest bank), zygites (middle bank) and thranites (upper bank). The thranites had the most exposed and physically demanding position, pulling the longest oars. The rest of the crew included ten to twenty hoplites stationed on deck to repel boarders, a handful of archers, the ship&rsquo;s officers, and the keleustes (the stroke-caller), who beat time to keep the oarsmen synchronised. The trireme was a precision instrument that required continuous training to operate effectively.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: how long was a trireme, how many rowers did it carry, what was its primary weapon, and what did each tier of rowers do?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A trireme was roughly 37 metres long with three banks of oars rowed by around 170 men in three tiers. It was built for speed and fought by ramming: the bronze ram at the prow was its primary weapon. Operating one required constant training.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Ramming Tactics</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Naval battles in this period were decided not by cannon or archery from a distance but by <dfn class="term" data-def="The primary tactic of trireme warfare, in which a warship drove its bronze ram into the side or stern of an enemy vessel to punch a hole in the hull and sink or disable it.">ramming</dfn>. A trireme aimed to hit an enemy ship at high speed, driving the bronze ram into the hull below the waterline to hole it and sink it, or to sweep along the side and shear off the oar blades &mdash; leaving the enemy crew unable to manoeuvre or escape. For these manoeuvres to work, the rowers needed near-perfect coordination and the helmsman exceptional skill.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The two main attack techniques were the <em>diekplous</em> &mdash; driving through a gap in the enemy line and then turning sharply to ram from behind &mdash; and the <em>periplous</em> &mdash; rowing around the end of the enemy line to attack from the flank or rear. Both required sea room to manoeuvre. Themistocles designed his trap at Salamis precisely around this: lure the larger Persian fleet into a narrow channel where their numerical advantage became worthless, and where the Greeks&rsquo; superior training would tell.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Strategy: Luring Xerxes into the Strait</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Themistocles&rsquo; strategic genius lay in recognising that the Greeks could not fight the Persians fleet to fleet in open water: Persian numbers would simply overwhelm them. The narrow strait between Salamis and the mainland, however, was roughly a kilometre wide at its narrowest point &mdash; not enough room for a large fleet to deploy freely or to use the periplous effectively.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12">According to Herodotus, Themistocles sent a message to Xerxes through a trusted slave, claiming that the Greek fleet was about to flee and that Xerxes should attack immediately to trap it. Xerxes took the bait, ordering his fleet to block all exits from the strait during the night. When dawn came, the cramped Persian fleet was already exhausted from rowing all night, while the Greeks had rested. The Persians found themselves unable to manoeuvre in the confined space. Greek triremes crashed through their lines, ramming and turning.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">The Battle and Artemisia</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The battle of Salamis took place in September 480 BC. Xerxes watched from a golden throne set up on the slopes of Mount Aigalos overlooking the strait. What he saw was the steady destruction of his fleet. The Persian ships, too many for the confined space, were bunched together. When one was rammed and began to sink, it blocked the manoeuvres of others. The Greeks, fighting in organised lines, pushed the Persians back towards their own shore.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">One of the most striking figures at Salamis in ancient accounts was <dfn class="term" data-def="Queen of Halicarnassus and ally of Xerxes who commanded ships at Salamis; Herodotus presents her as one of the most capable commanders in the Persian fleet, remarkable because she was a woman.">Artemisia</dfn>, the queen of Halicarnassus, who commanded her own ships in the Persian fleet. Herodotus reports that Xerxes admired her competence above all his other commanders. At Salamis, finding her ship under pursuit, she rammed another Persian vessel &mdash; possibly to escape or because she mistook it for a Greek ship. Xerxes, watching from the shore, saw what looked like an act of great boldness. The Athenian ship pursuing her broke off, thinking that Artemisia must be a Greek ally. She escaped unharmed.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain Themistocles' trap at Salamis: what did he do, why did it work, and what was the result?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Themistocles tricked Xerxes into sending his fleet into the narrow strait at night, exhausting his rowers while the Greeks rested. At dawn, the cramped Persian fleet could not manoeuvre; the more agile Greek triremes rammed through their lines and destroyed hundreds of ships. Xerxes watched his fleet collapse from a hilltop throne.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Visual Evidence: the Lenormant Trireme Relief</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A marble relief from the Acropolis at Athens, showing the prow and rowers of a trireme in remarkable detail; one of the most important pieces of visual evidence for the design and operation of the ancient warship.">Lenormant Trireme Relief</dfn> is a fragment of carved marble, found on the Acropolis at Athens and now in the Acropolis Museum. It shows the upper portion of a trireme&rsquo;s prow, with the thranites (upper-tier oarsmen) visible. The rowers are shown hunched forward, pulling hard. The detail is remarkable: individual oar-handles are visible, and the relative position of the rowers in their cramped benches gives a real sense of the physical demand of trireme warfare. A helmsman figure appears at the stern. The relief probably dated from around the late fifth century BC and was commissioned to celebrate Athenian naval power.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">What the Lenormant relief makes vivid is the human reality of trireme warfare: victory depended not on brave hoplites in shining armour but on the coordinated muscle and discipline of hundreds of ordinary men, most of them the poorest citizens of Athens. Their contribution to Salamis gave them a political claim that proved impossible to ignore: after the Persian Wars, Athenian democracy deepened significantly, in part because of what the fleet rowers had done.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Significance of Salamis for Athens and Greece</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">After Salamis, Xerxes withdrew to Persia, leaving his general Mardonius in Greece with a substantial land force. But without the fleet to supply and support it, that army was doomed. It was defeated at the land battle of Plataea in 479 BC, effectively ending the Persian invasion. Salamis was therefore the decisive turning point: the land victory at Plataea could not have followed without it.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21">For Athens, the victory reinforced the centrality of sea power to Athenian identity and empire. The Persian Wars became the founding narrative of the Athenian fifth-century golden age &mdash; the period that produced the Parthenon, the tragedies of Sophocles and the philosophy of Socrates. Salamis was commemorated in art, drama and poetry as proof that Athens had saved Greece, a claim the Athenians never let the rest of Greece forget.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The trireme was built for speed and ramming: roughly 37 metres long, with three banks of oars and a crew of about 200; its bronze ram was its primary weapon and victory depended on the coordination of trained oarsmen.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Themistocles tricked the Persian fleet into the narrow strait at Salamis, where the cramped conditions neutralised Persian numbers; the Greek victory in September 480 BC was the decisive turning point of the Persian Wars.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">The Lenormant Trireme Relief from the Acropolis is the most important visual source for trireme design, showing cramped oarsmen at their benches and illustrating how Athenian sea power rested on ordinary citizens.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">When analysing the Lenormant Trireme Relief, be specific: describe what is shown (thranites visible, cramped rowers, detail of oar-handles), say what it is (a marble relief from the Acropolis), and then explain what it reveals about trireme warfare (the dependence on trained, coordinated oarsmen rather than individual heroics; the physical demand of the work; the significance for Athenian democratic politics). For essay questions on Salamis, structure your answer around an argument &mdash; for example, whether tactics, geography or leadership was most decisive &mdash; and make sure you use named sources (Lenormant relief, Herodotus on Themistocles) rather than simply retelling events.</p>

